// M1 — Marketing and generative AI — Session 1. Market and target (segmentation, targeting, personas)
$CATEGORY: Master/Marketing/1. Market and target

::Running frequency::Grouping runners by how often and where they run is an example of which segmentation basis?{=Behavioural ~Demographic ~Geographic ~Psychographic}

::Values and lifestyle::Grouping consumers by their values and lifestyle, for example their commitment to sustainability, is an example of which segmentation basis?{=Psychographic ~Behavioural ~Demographic ~Geographic}

::Effective segments::Which of these is NOT a usual condition for an effective market segment?{=It includes every potential customer of the category ~It can be measured ~It can be reached through the company's channels ~It is large or profitable enough to serve}

::The T of STP::In the STP approach (segmentation, targeting, positioning), what does targeting mean?{=Choosing which segment or segments the company will serve ~Dividing the market into groups of buyers ~Deciding how the product should be perceived ~Setting the launch price}

::Attitude and behaviour::What does the attitude–behaviour gap mean for a sustainable product?{=Many consumers say they value sustainability more than they act on it when they buy ~Consumers who run more buy more shoes ~Younger consumers always pay more for sustainable products ~Consumers forget a brand after a campaign ends}

::Persona::What is a persona in marketing?{=A fictional but evidence-based portrait of a typical customer in the target segment ~A famous person who endorses the brand ~The total number of customers in a segment ~The legal entity that sells the product}
